{
  "model": "small",
  "language": "en",
  "text": "8.4 Why is Space Junk a problem? Well, the pieces of Space Junk travel at extremely fast speeds, over 25,000 km per hour, and so they can cause very bad problems if they hit anything. NASA, the US Space Agency, watches where the Space Junk is. The International Space Station, the ISS, can move out of the way of the Space Junk if they get a message to move at least five hours before an accident. Because people continue to send so many things into space, and this first started in 1957, there are now over 27,000 pieces of Space Junk. The smaller pieces might be as tiny as a bit of paint, but most of them are larger than 10 cm across. These are parts of old spacecraft, broken pieces of satellites, and other things. Some of this happened because of accidents in space, which can create thousands of pieces of Space Junk. Many people would like to find a solution to Space Junk if they could. Scientists worry that if large pieces of Space Junk hit each other, they would break, and there would be more and more broken pieces of Space Junk. When there is too much Space Junk, we won't be able to use the space around the Earth for satellites. So, what options do we have to solve the problem? The British mission, Remove Debris, is trying to solve the problem by catching Space Junk and taking it away. If all the Space Junk was gone, there wouldn't be a problem anymore. But what about Space Junk falling from space? What is the danger to people? There's not much chance anything would hit you if you were outside on a normal day. That's because most junk burns as it falls to Earth. In 1997, one person in the US was hit by a piece of Space Junk, and she was not hurt at all. In 2022, a large piece of Space Junk from a SpaceX spacecraft fell to the surface of the Earth and landed on an Australian sheep farm. From time to time, other large pieces hit the ground, usually in rural areas. These days, most space agencies work to make their old spacecraft fall in the ocean, if possible. However, with tens of thousands of pieces of Space Junk, there might be more than rain falling from the sky.",
